    "title": "What channel is Michigan vs Alabama basketball today? Time, TV schedule to watch Sweet 16 game",
    "text": "March 27, 2026, 1:06 p.m. ETThe second game of Sweet 16 action on Friday, March 27 will feature two teams with interesting parallels among their two coaches.Dusty May and No. 1 seed Michigan will face Nate Oats and No. 4 seed Alabama in the Midwest Regional semifinal with an Elite Eight berth up for grabs. Both May and Oats have ties to basketball in Michigan before their current roles, with the former serving as an assistant at Eastern Michigan in 2005-06 and Oats getting his start in the sport coaching Romulus High School in Detroit.Of course, May and Oats have also led Final Four-bound teams (May with Florida Atlantic in 2023 and Oats with Alabama in 2024).Watch select March Madness games live with Sling TVMichigan, led by UAB transfer Yaxel Lendeborg, largely dominated its competition in the opening weekend, crushing No. 16 seed Howard and No. 9 seed Saint Louis by an average score of 22 points per game.Alabama has similarly cruised through its opening games, downing No. 13 seed Hofstra and No. 5 seed Texas Tech and winning by 22.5 points per game with Labaron Philon leading the way.Here's how to watch as Michigan and Alabama face off in the Sweet 16, including game time, TV and streaming information, odds and more:What channel is Michigan vs Alabama basketball on today in Sweet 16?TV: TBS/truTVStreaming: NCAA March Madness Live | Sling TVMichigan and Alabama's Sweet 16 game will air nationally on TBS, with a simulcast on truTV. Andrew Catalon, Steve Lappas and Evan Washburn will be on the call.Streaming options for the game include NCAA March Madness Live on the NCAA's website or Sling TV, the latter of which carries TBS and truTV.Stream Michigan vs Alabama live with Sling TVMichigan-Alabama Sweet 16 game time todayDate: Friday, March 27Time: 7:35 p.m. ET | 6:35 p.m. CTLocation: United Center (Chicago)The Wolverines-Crimson Tide game is scheduled to tip off on March 27 at 7:35 p.m. ET (6:35 p.m. CT) at the United Center in Chicago.Michigan vs Alabama picks, predictions, odds in Sweet 16Odds courtesy of BetMGM as of 12:30 p.m. ET on March 27Odds: Michigan (-8.5)Over/under: 172.5Moneyline: Michigan (-500) | Alabama (+375)PredictionsJohn Leuzzi: MichiganJordan Mendoza: MichiganEhsan Kassim: AlabamaAustin Curtright: MichiganOur team of savvy editors independently handpicks all recommendations. If you purchase through our links, the USA TODAY Network may earn a commission. Prices were accurate at the time of publication but may change.Gambling involves risk. Please only gamble with funds that you can comfortably afford to lose. While we do our utmost to offer good advice and information we cannot be held responsible for any loss that may be incurred as a result of gambling. We do our best to make sure all the information that we provide on this site is correct. However, from time to time mistakes will be made and we will not be held liable. Please check any stats or information if you are unsure how accurate they are. No guarantees are made with regards to results or financial gain. All forms of betting carry financial risk and it is up to the individual to make bets with or without the assistance of information provided on this site and we cannot be held responsible for any loss that may be incurred as a result of following the betting tips provided on this site. Past performances do not guarantee success in the future and betting odds fluctuate from one minute to the next. The material contained on this site is intended to inform, entertain and educate the reader and in no way represents an inducement to gamble legally or illegally or any sort of professional advice.Gannett may earn revenue from sports betting operators for audience referrals to betting services. Sports betting operators have no influence over nor are any such revenues in any way dependent on or linked to the newsrooms or news coverage. Terms apply, see operator site for Terms and Conditions. If you or someone you know has a gambling problem, he